# LugalOS System Programmer Manual

Documentation > System Programmer Manual

Welcome to the LugalOS System Programmer Manual. This guide provides comprehensive technical documentation for software developers building applications for LugalOS as well as kernel engineers extending the operating system.

## Manual Structure

```
doc/dev/
|-- on_device/          # Part A: User-Space Application Development
|   |-- c_programming.md   # C11 programming with chibicc, lugal.h, syscall ABI, ELF
|   \-- lisp_scripting.md  # Lisp machine runtime, zero-SRAM builtins, stdlib, CAS
|-- kernel/             # Part B: LugalOS Kernel & Driver Architecture
|   |-- architecture.md    # Microkernel model, memory domains (PMP/Sv39), IPC channels
|   |-- scheduler.md       # Priority tiers, preemption, cross-hart lock hand-off, SMP
|   |-- concurrency.md     # Ownership comments, racescan, locks and lock order, waits, stress
|   |-- logging.md         # Tokenized kernel logging macro system, klogd, rodata dictionary
|   |-- drivers.md         # Confined U-mode driver framework, PMP register grants, IRQs
|   \-- porting.md         # Board bringup: linker scripts, clock trees, XIP flash
|-- protocols/          # Part D: Interconnect & Wire Protocols
|   \-- llp.md             # LugalOS Link Protocol (LLP): framing, flow control, 9P/Ethernet mux
\-- testing.md          # Part C: Testing Framework & QA
    |-- Unit tests & pure algorithmic verifiers
    |-- Host harnesses with ASan/UBSan (FAT32, cc, 9P, IP)
    |-- Automated QEMU regression runner (tests/runner.py)
    \-- Hardware-in-the-loop silicon suites (tests/hw/)
```

## 1. Part A: On-Device Application Development
* C Programming on LugalOS: Writing native C programs compiled either on the host or directly on the device using chibicc. Covers lugal.h, available standard library functions, process arguments, and U-mode memory confinement.
* Lisp Scripting & Language Extensions: LugalOS Lisp language reference, special forms, zero-SRAM builtins in Flash .rodata, modular packages (seq, dict, matrix), and scripting the CAS.

## 2. Part B: LugalOS Kernel & Driver Architecture
* Microkernel Architecture: Hardware-isolated tasks, PMP regions on NOMMU, Sv39 page tables on MMU, copy-always channels (chan_t), and the syscall validation boundary.
* Scheduler & Multicore SMP: Preemptive round-robin scheduler with static priority tiers, atomic primitives (spinlockt, ylockt), the critical cross-hart scheduler lock hand-off across ctx_switch(), and Lazy SMP.
* Concurrency: Shared State, Locks and Waits: The ownership comment on every shared static and racescan.py, spinlocks vs ylocks and the learned lock order, chancallwait() and timed polls, and the stress harness.
* Tokenized Kernel Logging Macro System: Zero-overhead logging capturing format-string addresses in .rodata and vararg binary blobs in the ring buffer, expanded on drain by klogd.
* Driver Framework & Fault Isolation: Running drivers as confined U-mode tasks with fine-grained PMP hardware register grants and asynchronous message queues.
* Board Porting Guide: Step-by-step instructions for porting LugalOS to new RISC-V microcontrollers, clock trees, and XIP memory layouts.

## 3. Part C: Testing Framework & QA
* Testing Guide: How to run and extend the LugalOS test suite across unit tests, host harnesses with AddressSanitizer, automated QEMU regression tests, and physical hardware-in-the-loop tests.

## 4. Part D: Interconnect & Wire Protocols
* Protected 9P sessions: Integrity for 9P over TCP and WebSocket without TLS: the 9P2000.lp version, the session key derived from the grant key and the auth challenge, the per-frame tag, test vectors, and why secrets still stay off a readable link.
* LugalOS Link Protocol (LLP): Point-to-point interconnect protocol multiplexing 9P filesystem calls and 802.3 Ethernet frames between coprocessors (e.g. ESP32-P4 and ESP32-C6) with credit-based flow control, session negotiation, and link liveness.

